<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Gaozu of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded dynasty <a href="/years/618">618 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Yuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Gaozu, born Li Yuan, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> general, he rebelled during the collapse of the Sui and founded the dynasty in 618 CE. He abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, the future Emperor Taizong, after the Xuanwu Gate incident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Gaozu of Tang" width="204" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TangGaozu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Yuan was born on 7 April 566. According to the official genealogy of the Tang ruling house, his ancestor in the seventh generation was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-gao">Li Gao</a>, founder of the state of Western Liang. His grandfather Li Hu served under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> and was given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> surname Daye. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bing">Li Bing</a> died in 572, when Li Yuan inherited the title of Duke of Tang.</p><p>He kept the title after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> throne in 581 as Emperor Wen of Sui, whose wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dugu">Empress Dugu</a>, was Li Yuan's aunt. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> he became governor in the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, and in 615 he was sent to garrison Longxi, where he gained experience in dealing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> grew dissatisfied with Li Yuan over his inability to stop incursions by the Eastern Turks and the growing strength of agrarian rebels. Li Yuan was also fearful because of prophecies that the next emperor would be named Li. Traditional accounts, compiled under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, stress the son's role in urging the rebellion, but modern researchers have concluded that the initiative came from Li Yuan himself. He rose in July 617 and took the throne in 618 after Emperor Yang was killed at Jiangdu.</p><p>Gaozu's reign was spent uniting the empire. Aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, whom he made Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, he defeated rivals including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengao">Xue Rengao</a>, Liu Wuzhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a>, and by 628 the Tang had united all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He restored much of the institutions of Emperor Wen, including the equal distribution of land, lowered taxes, and abandoned the harsh legal system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A rivalry grew between the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, who had the greater reputation in the army. In 626 Li Shimin ambushed and killed Li Jiancheng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanji">Li Yuanji</a> at the Xuanwu Gate, and Gaozu passed the throne to him, becoming Taishang Huang, or retired emperor.</p><p>He exerted little influence afterwards, and in 630, hearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> had defeated the Turkic khagan Jiali Khan, he held a celebration and played the pipa himself. He died on 25 June 635, before a summer palace that Taizong had begun for him was finished. His son honoured him as Gaozu, meaning high founder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Gaozu of Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the north the aristocrat <b>Li Yuan</b> rose and ascended the throne as Emperor Gaozu of Tang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang Dynasty was founded in 618 CE by <b>Li Yuan</b> (Emperor Gaozu), Duke of Tang and a Sui military commander at Taiyuan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling houses of the Northern Zhou, Sui and Tang came from a mixed Xianbei and Chinese aristocracy of the north-west: Empress Dugu, wife of the Sui founder, and the mother of the Tang founder <b>Li Yuan</b> were daughters of the Xianbei general Dugu Xin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Sui, <b>Li Yuan</b> took the throne at Chang'an as Emperor Gaozu.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Li Shimin, who had encouraged his father <b>Li Yuan</b>'s rebellion against the Sui, took the throne as the second Tang emperor, known after his death as Taizong.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebel Duke of Tang, <b>Li Yuan</b>, advancing on the Sui capital, routed an elite Sui army of 20,000 at Huoyi after an initial setback.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-8">8 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=618" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emperor Gaozu of Tang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4218 4594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4218 4594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=618">What was here in 618 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
